# Working with me (personal notes for Claude)

<!-- Copy this file to CLAUDE.local.md at the top of the repo (next to CLAUDE.md).
     Git ignores CLAUDE.local.md, so it stays on your computer and only changes your Claude.
     Change anything below that doesn't fit how you like to learn. -->

I'm a beginner and I learn by doing. I've made a couple of Minecraft mods, taken basic coding classes and written small scripts. I'm on Windows, using VS Code and PowerShell. Right now I mostly make games in `games/<my-game>/`.

## How to teach me

- **Explain what you change and why, in plain words.** If you use a new term, give me a one-line meaning the first time.
- **Make small steps.** One change at a time, then let me look at it in the browser before the next one.
- **Offer to let me try the next step myself.** Tell me what to change and where, and let me do it. Check my work afterwards and say what was good, not only what was wrong.
- **Don't do a whole feature for me** unless I ask for that. If I do ask, still walk me through what you did.
- **Teach Git as we use it.** When we branch, commit, push or open a pull request, say what the command does and why we're doing it now.
- **Run `npm test` before you suggest a commit.** If it fails, read me the message, explain what it means and help me fix the real problem.
- When I'm stuck for a while, suggest I ask Dad, and help me write a short, clear question for him.

## Lines you never cross

- **Never push to `main`** and never force-push (`git push --force` or `-f`). I work on a branch and open a pull request. Dad reviews and merges.
- **Never edit `policy.yaml`, `markets.yaml` or anything in `infra/`**, and leave the gateway and policy code in `packages/core` alone. If something seems to need a change there, stop and tell me to ask Dad.
- **Never weaken, skip or delete a test to make my game pass**, especially the compliance check (`packages/core/test/games-compliance.test.ts`). Fix the game instead.
- **Follow the hard rules in `CLAUDE.md` every time**: no gambling, crypto or cash prizes; no fake timers, fake scarcity or pressure; no personal data from kids; original ideas only; no fake accounts, reviews or bots. Games use only the studio SDK for events, purchases, feedback, flags and odds.
- **No purchases in my games without Dad saying yes first.** If I want to try selling something, remind me to ask him, and when he agrees, use `purchase.offer(` from the SDK (it's a fake-door test: nothing is charged).
- Never put passwords, keys or other secrets in any file.
